    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Glyphosate, originally patented as a metal chelator, is a primary active ingredient in many herbicides used worldwide. While glyphosate alone is thought to be non-toxic, the possibility that its metal-chelating properties could contribute to its toxicity in vivo has not been tested. Normally, strong chelators cause bound metals to be inert, and therefore easy to dispel. However, a weaker chelator such as glyphosate could allow metal ions to pass into the body until the complex reaches a region with low pH, which can lower the stability of the metal-chelator complex in vitro. With a weaker metal-chelator bond, the complex may dissociate, leaving the heavy metal ions in the tissue, potentially causing toxicity. However, there is a lack of in vivo systems to test this hypothesis. Because of their responsiveness to metal stress, C. elegans have proven useful as environmental biosensors. For this work, a collection of transgenic GFP (green fluorescent protein) reporter strains was generated utilizing CRISPR/Cas9 to study the hypothesis. Each strain bears GFP (green fluorescent protein) under the regulation of genes involved in the metal stress response pathway and exhibit dynamic metal- and dose-dependent GFP induction. The strains were then exposed to varying conditions of both metal and chelating agents at low and high pH. The results show an ability to quantify modest and dose-dependent changes in metal response in living animals, and hence to evaluate conditions under which the weak chelator might potentiate effects seen with metal-only conditions. This work establishes a technical foundation for further study of chelation-dependent effects of metal deposition and toxicity. Future research may provide valuable insight into the potentially toxic interactions of the widely-used herbicide ingredient, glyphosate, and demonstrates the need for further investigation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
